  "title": "Why leader election fails on cloud networks",
  "content": "The vendor docs cover the setup and go quiet on the failure modes. Inherited offlinefirst eventual setups tend to come with no explanation, just a working system and a long reverse-engineering session. Here's the fuller picture, with the tradeoffs left in.\n\nLeader election looks simple on paper: a set of nodes agrees on one leader, and if the leader dies, another takes over. In practice, on modern cloud networks, the failure modes are subtle and often invisible until they cause a split brain, a stalled cluster, or a leader that keeps getting elected and deposed in a tight loop. This post walks through why those failures happen, the approaches that commonly break, and a practical pattern for making leader election more resilient. By the end, you'll know which metrics to watch and which configuration knobs to turn first.\n\n## The problem, in general terms\n\nLeader election is a consensus problem. Distributed consensus algorithms like Raft and Paxos are designed to tolerate crash failures and network partitions, but they rely on assumptions about the network that cloud environments violate in ways that are easy to miss. The core assumption is that message delivery is either timely or not delivered at all—there is no notion of a message that arrives very late. In cloud networks, packets can be delayed by hundreds of milliseconds or even seconds due to congestion, retries, or virtual network quirks, and they can also be reordered or duplicated. These delays can cause a follower to believe the leader is dead when it is merely slow, triggering an election that shouldn't happen.\n\nAnother assumption is that clocks are roughly synchronized. Cloud providers offer time synchronization services, but clock drift still occurs, and algorithms that use timeouts to detect failures are sensitive to it. If a node's clock jumps forward, it may time out a healthy leader prematurely. If it jumps backward, it may wait too long to detect a real failure.\n\nThe third assumption is that the network is homogeneous and stable. Cloud networks are not: they span multiple availability zones with varying latency, they have noisy neighbors, and they can experience transient packet loss during live migrations or host maintenance. These variations mean that a timeout value that works in one part of the network may be too aggressive or too lax in another.\n\n## The approaches that commonly fail, and why\n\n### Naive heartbeat with fixed timeout\n\nThe simplest approach is for each node to send heartbeats to others and declare the leader dead if no heartbeat is received within a fixed timeout. This fails when the timeout is too short: a temporary network hiccup or a GC pause on the leader causes followers to start an election. If multiple followers time out simultaneously, they may split the vote, leading to no leader or frequent leader churn. If the timeout is too long, failover takes too long, and the system is unavailable for that duration. Finding a fixed timeout that works across all conditions is nearly impossible in a cloud environment.\n\n### Using a single external lock service\n\nMany teams use a distributed lock service like etcd, Consul, or ZooKeeper to hold the leader lock. While these systems implement consensus internally, using them as a lock service introduces a new dependency: the lock service itself must be highly available. If the lock service becomes unreachable due to a network partition, the application cannot determine whether the leader is still alive. Some implementations use a lease with a TTL, but if the application cannot renew the lease due to network issues, it may lose leadership even though it is healthy. Worse, if the lock service is partitioned, two applications might both believe they hold the lock if the lease expires and they re-acquire without proper fencing.\n\n### Relying on cloud provider instance metadata\n\nSome designs use cloud provider APIs (like EC2 instance status checks) to determine if a node is healthy. These APIs are eventually consistent and can lag behind reality. A node might be marked unhealthy while it is still running and serving traffic, leading to unnecessary failover. Conversely, a node might be marked healthy after it has actually failed, delaying failover.\n\n### Ignoring clock skew\n\nAlgorithms that use wall-clock time for lease expiration are vulnerable to clock skew. Even with NTP, clocks can drift by milliseconds to seconds. If a leader's clock is slow, it may think its lease is still valid while followers with faster clocks think it has expired. This can lead to two leaders acting simultaneously.\n\n## The approach that works in practice\n\nThe most robust approach is to use a consensus protocol like Raft, which is designed to handle these issues, but to configure it carefully and to add safeguards. Raft uses a randomized election timeout to reduce split votes, and it uses a term number to ensure that only one leader is elected per term. However, Raft alone does not solve the problem of network delays causing unnecessary elections; it just makes them less likely to cause inconsistency.\n\nA practical pattern is to combine Raft with a lease-based mechanism that is resilient to clock skew. Instead of relying on wall-clock time, use a monotonic clock for timeouts and lease durations. Monotonic clocks are not affected by NTP adjustments and only move forward. In Go, for example, the `time` package provides monotonic clock readings when you use `time.Now()` and compare times with `Sub` or `Before`. In Java, `System.nanoTime()` provides a monotonic clock. This ensures that a node's timeout calculations are not skewed by clock adjustments.\n\nAnother key practice is to use a quorum-based approach for leader election, where a leader must maintain contact with a majority of nodes to remain leader. This is what Raft does. But you can also implement a lightweight version using a gossip protocol and a failure detector that adapts to network conditions. For example, the SWIM protocol (used by HashiCorp's memberlist) uses indirect probing and suspicion to reduce false positives. However, SWIM is not a consensus protocol; it's a membership protocol, so it doesn't provide strong consistency for leader election. It can be used to detect failures, but you still need a consensus layer to agree on the leader.\n\nA more practical approach for many applications is to use a managed coordination service that handles these complexities, such as AWS's managed ZooKeeper or etcd, but to design your application to tolerate the loss of the coordination service. This means implementing a fallback: if the coordination service is unreachable, the current leader continues to act as leader for a bounded period (a lease), and followers do not attempt to elect a new leader until that lease expires. This is essentially a lease-based leader election with a grace period. The lease duration should be longer than the maximum expected network partition duration, but short enough to ensure timely failover. A common choice is 10-30 seconds, but this depends on your recovery time objective.\n\n## Implementation details\n\nLet's look at a minimal implementation of a lease-based leader election using etcd. The idea is that each candidate tries to create a lease and then a key with that lease. The first one to succeed becomes leader. The leader must periodically renew the lease. If the leader fails to renew, the lease expires and the key is deleted, allowing another candidate to become leader. To avoid split brain due to network partitions, the leader should also check that it can still reach a quorum of etcd nodes before performing actions. However, etcd itself uses Raft, so if the leader can renew its lease, it means it can reach a quorum of etcd nodes. The risk is that the leader might be partitioned from the etcd cluster but still think it is leader because its local lease cache hasn't expired. To mitigate this, the leader should use a short lease TTL and renew frequently, and it should also verify leadership by reading the key back with a quorum read (using the `WithSerializable` option would not be sufficient; you need a linearizable read). In etcd's Go client, you can use `client.Get(ctx, key, clientv3.WithSerializable())` for serializable reads, but for linearizable you omit that option. A linearizable read ensures that the response is from the leader and that it has quorum.\n\nHere is a simplified example in Go:\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"context\"\n    \"log\"\n    \"time\"\n\n    clientv3 \"go.etcd.io/etcd/client/v3\"\n    \"go.etcd.io/etcd/client/v3/concurrency\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n    cli, err := clientv3.New(clientv3.Config{\n        Endpoints:   []string{\"localhost:2379\"},\n        DialTimeout: 5 * time.Second,\n    })\n    if err != nil {\n        log.Fatal(err)\n    }\n    defer cli.Close()\n\n    session, err := concurrency.NewSession(cli, concurrency.WithTTL(10))\n    if err != nil {\n        log.Fatal(err)\n    }\n    defer session.Close()\n\n    election := concurrency.NewElection(session, \"/myapp/leader\")\n    ctx := context.Background()\n\n    // Campaign blocks until we become leader or ctx is canceled.\n    if err := election.Campaign(ctx, \"candidate1\"); err != nil {\n        log.Fatal(err)\n    }\n\n    log.Println(\"I am the leader\")\n\n    // Do leader work here. Periodically check leadership by attempting a\n    // linearizable read of the leader key.\n    ticker := time.NewTicker(2 * time.Second)\n    defer ticker.Stop()\n    for {\n        select {\n        case <-ticker.C:\n            // Verify we are still leader with a linearizable read.\n            resp, err := cli.Get(ctx, \"/myapp/leader\", clientv3.WithFirstCreate()...)\n            if err != nil {\n                log.Println(\"Lost leadership:\", err)\n                return\n            }\n            if string(resp.Kvs[0].Value) != \"candidate1\" {\n                log.Println(\"Lost leadership: another candidate is leader\")\n                return\n            }\n        case <-session.Done():\n            log.Println(\"Session expired, lost leadership\")\n            return\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis example uses the etcd concurrency package, which implements leader election using leases. The `session` has a TTL of 10 seconds, and it automatically sends keepalives. If the session expires, the `session.Done()` channel is closed, and the leader should stop acting as leader. The periodic linearizable read of the leader key ensures that the leader is still the one holding the key, even if its session hasn't expired yet (e.g., due to a network partition where the leader can't reach etcd but its local session hasn't timed out). This is a critical safeguard against split brain.\n\n## What outcomes to expect, and their limits\n\nWith a properly configured lease-based election, you can expect failover to occur within the lease TTL plus the time for a new leader to campaign. With a 10-second TTL, failover typically happens within 10-15 seconds. However, this is not instantaneous, and during that window, the system may be unavailable for writes. If your application requires sub-second failover, you need a different approach, such as a primary-backup replication with synchronous replication and a hot standby that can take over quickly, but that comes with its own trade-offs.\n\nAlso, the lease-based approach relies on the coordination service being available. If the etcd cluster loses quorum, no new leader can be elected, and the existing leader will lose its lease after the TTL. This means that a quorum loss in the coordination service leads to a complete outage of leader election. To mitigate this, some teams use a multi-layer approach: a local leader election within a partition, and a global leader election across partitions, with reconciliation. That's complex and rarely needed.\n\nThe limits are clear: you cannot have both strong consistency and high availability during a network partition. You must choose. Most systems choose consistency (CP) for leader election, accepting that during a partition, the minority partition cannot elect a leader. This is the correct choice for most applications.\n\n## What to watch out for\n\n- **Clock skew**: Use monotonic clocks for timeouts. Ensure NTP is configured and monitored. In Kubernetes, the `node` clock is usually synchronized, but if you run on VMs, check `chronyd` or `systemd-timesyncd`.\n- **Network latency variability**: Set timeouts based on observed p99 latency, not average. Use adaptive timeouts if possible. For example, etcd's election timeout is randomized between 1000-2000ms by default, but you can tune it. However, tuning etcd's internal timeouts is rarely necessary; instead, tune your application's lease TTL.\n- **GC pauses**: A long GC pause on the leader can cause it to miss heartbeats. Use a lease TTL that is longer than the maximum GC pause. For JVM applications, monitor GC pauses and set the lease TTL accordingly. For Go, GC pauses are usually sub-millisecond, but still consider them.\n- **Fencing tokens**: Even with a lease, there is a window where an old leader might still be acting while a new leader has been elected. Use fencing tokens (a monotonically increasing number) to ensure that downstream systems reject requests from an old leader. For example, when writing to a database, include the fencing token in the write and have the database reject writes with a token lower than the highest seen.\n- **Monitoring**: Track leader changes, election duration, and lease renewal failures. Alert on frequent leader changes (e.g., more than 1 per hour) as it indicates instability.\n\n## The broader lesson\n\nLeader election is a distributed consensus problem, and consensus is hard. The cloud network introduces variability that breaks simplistic assumptions. The key principle is to design for failure: assume that network partitions will happen, that clocks will skew, and that messages will be delayed. Use proven algorithms (Raft) and battle-tested implementations (etcd, Consul) rather than rolling your own. But even with those, you must understand the failure modes and add safeguards like fencing tokens and monotonic clocks. The broader lesson is that in distributed systems, the network is not reliable, and any algorithm that assumes otherwise will eventually fail in subtle ways.\n\n## How to apply this to your situation\n\nStart by auditing your current leader election mechanism. If you're using a fixed timeout, replace it with a lease-based approach using a coordination service. If you're already using etcd or Consul, check that your lease TTL is appropriate for your network conditions and that you're using linearizable reads to verify leadership. Add fencing tokens to your writes to prevent split brain. And monitor leader changes—if you see frequent changes, investigate network latency and GC pauses.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### Why does my leader keep changing every few minutes?\n\nFrequent leader changes usually indicate that the lease is expiring prematurely. Common causes include network latency spikes causing missed heartbeats, GC pauses on the leader, or clock skew causing the leader to think its lease is still valid while followers disagree. Check the coordination service logs for lease expiration events and correlate with network and GC metrics. Increasing the lease TTL can help, but first fix the underlying cause.\n\n### Can I use a database as a lock service for leader election?\n\nUsing a relational database with a table and row-level locking is possible but risky. Databases are not designed for high-frequency lease renewals, and they may not provide linearizable reads under all isolation levels. For example, PostgreSQL's default isolation level (Read Committed) does not prevent phantom reads, and you need to use `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` carefully. If the database is partitioned, you may end up with two leaders. It's better to use a dedicated coordination service.\n\n### How do I test leader election under network partitions?\n\nUse a tool like `tc` (traffic control) on Linux to introduce network delays and packet loss, or use a chaos engineering tool like Chaos Mesh to simulate partitions. Run your cluster with three or five nodes and introduce a partition between the leader and the followers. Verify that a new leader is elected and that the old leader stops acting as leader within the lease TTL. Also test clock skew by adjusting the system clock on one node (in a test environment) and observing the behavior.\n\n### What is a fencing token and why do I need it?\n\nA fencing token is a monotonically increasing number that is included with every write request. When a new leader is elected, it increments the token. Downstream systems (like databases or storage services) reject any write with a token lower than the highest seen. This prevents an old leader that is still running (e.g., due to a long GC pause) from making writes after a new leader has taken over. Without fencing tokens, you can have split brain even with a lease-based election.\n\n## Further reading\n\n- The Raft consensus algorithm paper by Diego Ongaro and John Ousterhout.\n- etcd documentation on leader election and leases.\n- The Google Chubby paper on lock service design.\n- AWS documentation on time synchronization for EC2 instances.\n\n## Action for the next 30 minutes\n\nOpen your leader election configuration file (e.g., `etcd.conf.yml` or your application's config) and check the lease TTL. If it's less than 10 seconds, consider increasing it to 10-15 seconds to tolerate network jitter and GC pauses. Then, add a linearizable read check in your leader loop to verify leadership before performing critical operations.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
